# create-official-documentation project-rule-parameter

## File Purpose and Relationship

This manual Project Rule defines the comprehensive creation protocol for new documentation files in the PolygonExpress project. It should be consulted when:

- Creating new documentation for components, features, or APIs
- Ensuring documentation meets project format standards 
- Following a standardized research-to-creation workflow
- Designing effective component diagrams and examples

This file is designed to be used as a project-rule-parameter with the `create-official-documentation` message-command:
```
create-official-documentation: {path} @manual/documentation/create-official-documentation.mdc
```

This file complements:
- **documentation-workflow.mdc**: Core documentation workflow process
- **global-rules.mdc**: Core identity and mode concepts
- **verify-official-documentation.mdc**: Documentation verification protocol

For specialized guidance, see:
- **update-documentation-format.mdc**: Format update and standardization guidelines

When information appears to conflict, this file takes precedence for documentation creation standards.

## Parameter Syntax and Usage

### Parameter Types

Documentation creation message-commands accept two distinct types of parameters:

1. **Message-Command Parameters**
   - Part of the message-command syntax itself
   - Specified directly after a colon in the message-command
   - Provides specific file path for creation
   - Example: `create-official-documentation: official-documentation/front-end/api-client.md`

2. **Project Rule Parameters**
   - Referenced Project Rules that provide creation guidance
   - Auto-attached when working with documentation files
   - Explicitly referenced using @rule-name when needed
   - Example: `create-official-documentation: official-documentation/front-end/api-client.md @manual/documentation/create-official-documentation.mdc`

### Multiple Parameter Syntax

When a message-command requires both message-command parameters and Project Rule parameters, use this format:

```
message-command: [message-command-parameter] @[project-rule-parameter]
```

**Examples:**
```
create-official-documentation: official-documentation/front-end/api-client.md @manual/documentation/create-official-documentation.mdc
create-official-documentation: official-documentation/blockchain/transaction-flow.md @manual/documentation/create-official-documentation.mdc
```

### Documentation Creation Parameter Format

| Message-Command | Parameter Format | Example | Purpose |
|-----------------|-----------------|---------|---------|
| `create-official-documentation` | File path | `create-official-documentation: official-documentation/front-end/api-client.md` | Create new documentation file at specified path |

## 1. Documentation Creation Protocol

### 5-Phase Creation Process

When executing ANY documentation creation message-command, you MUST follow this 5-phase creation process:

#### Phase 1: Research Phase
```typescript
# Step 1: Identify content to document
codebase_search("API client implementation", ["front-end/src/api"])
grep_search("class ApiClient", false, "*.ts")
file_search("ApiClient.ts")

# Step 2: Create implementation file list (mental process)
# - Identify primary implementation files
# - Note supporting files and dependencies
# - Map component relationships and hierarchy
```

#### Phase 2: Implementation Reading Phase
```typescript
# Step 1: Read primary implementation files completely
read_file("front-end/src/api/ApiClient.ts", should_read_entire_file=true)
read_file("front-end/src/api/endpoints.ts", should_read_entire_file=true)

# Step 2: Read supporting files as needed
read_file("front-end/src/types/api.ts", should_read_entire_file=true)
read_file("front-end/src/utils/apiHelpers.ts", should_read_entire_file=true)

# Step 3: Extract key information (mental process)
# - Identify core functionality and purpose
# - Catalog interfaces, functions, and patterns
# - Note error handling and edge cases
# - Map component relationships for diagrams
```

#### Phase 3: Format Research Phase
```typescript
# Step 1: Read documentation format standards
read_file("official-documentation/README.md", should_read_entire_file=true)

# Step 2: Review similar documentation for consistent style
read_file("official-documentation/front-end/components/structure.md", should_read_entire_file=true)

# Step 3: Analyze format requirements (mental process)
# - Identify required sections
# - Note component diagram patterns
# - Observe code example formats
# - Understand cross-referencing style
```

#### Phase 4: Creation Phase
```typescript
# Step 1: Create complete documentation file in a single operation
edit_file("official-documentation/front-end/api-client.md",
          "Create comprehensive API Client documentation",
          "# API Client\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe API Client provides a centralized interface for all API interactions in the PolygonExpress platform, handling request formatting, error management, and response parsing.\n\n## Component Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    ApiClient[API Client] --> Endpoints[API Endpoints]\n    ApiClient --> ErrorHandler[Error Handler]\n    ApiClient --> ResponseParser[Response Parser]\n    \n    Endpoints --> OrdersAPI[Orders API]\n    Endpoints --> UsersAPI[Users API]\n    Endpoints --> AuthAPI[Auth API]\n    \n    subgraph \"Core API Layer\"\n        ApiClient\n        Endpoints\n        ErrorHandler\n        ResponseParser\n    end\n    \n    subgraph \"Domain APIs\"\n        OrdersAPI\n        UsersAPI\n        AuthAPI\n    end\n```\n\n## Core Interfaces\n\n```typescript\ninterface ApiClientConfig {\n  baseUrl: string;\n  timeout: number;\n  retryCount: number;\n}\n\ninterface ApiResponse<T> {\n  data: T;\n  status: number;\n  headers: Record<string, string>;\n}\n\ninterface ApiError {\n  message: string;\n  code: string;\n  status: number;\n  originalError?: unknown;\n}\n```\n\n## Usage Example\n\n```typescript\nimport { apiClient } from '@/api';\n\n// Basic GET request\nconst fetchUsers = async () => {\n  try {\n    const response = await apiClient.get<User[]>('/users');\n    return response.data;\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error('Failed to fetch users:', error);\n    throw error;\n  }\n};\n\n// POST request with body\nconst createOrder = async (orderData: CreateOrderRequest) => {\n  try {\n    const response = await apiClient.post<Order>('/orders', orderData);\n    return response.data;\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error('Failed to create order:', error);\n    throw error;\n  }\n};\n```\n\n## Error Handling\n\nThe API Client provides standardized error handling with the following features:\n\n- Automatic retry for network failures (configured via `retryCount`)\n- Consistent error format across all API calls\n- Detailed error information including original error objects\n- HTTP status code categorization\n\n### Error Types\n\n| Status Code Range | Error Category | Retry Strategy |\n|-------------------|----------------|----------------|\n| 400-499 | Client Errors | No retry (except 408) |\n| 500-599 | Server Errors | Automatic retry with backoff |\n| Network | Connection Errors | Automatic retry with backoff |\n\n## Related Components\n\n- [Auth Service](./auth-service.md) - Handles authentication tokens\n- [Orders API](./orders-api.md) - Domain-specific API for orders\n- [API Types](./api-types.md) - Type definitions for API requests and responses")

# Step 2: Verify content organization and formatting (mental process)
